109: \nL110: cite60†Sign up L111: \nL112: Appearance settings\nL113: You signed in with another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You signed out in another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You switched accounts on another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. Dismiss alert\nL114: \nL115:   1. cite61†deepseek-ai L116:   2. cite62†LPLB L117: ## Repository navigation\nL118: \nL119:   *   * cite62†Code L120:   * cite63†Issues1 (1) L121:   * cite64†Pull requests1 (1) L122:   * cite65†Discussions L123:   * cite66†Actions L124:   * cite67†Security and quality L125:   * cite68†Insights L126: \nL127: More items\nL128: \nL129: cite69†Image: deepseek-ai†avatars.githubusercontent.com L130: \nL131: cite62†LPLB L132: \nL133: Public\nL134: \nL135:   * cite70†Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\nL136:   * cite70†Fork44 (44) L137:   * cite70†Star538 (538) You must be signed in to star a repository\nL138: ## About\nL139: \nL140: An early research stage expert-parallel load balancer for MoE models based on linear programming.\nL141: \nL142: ### Resources\nL143: \nL144: cite71†Readme L145: \nL146: cite72†MIT license L147: \nL148: cite73†Activity L149: \nL150: cite74†Custom properties L151: \nL152: ### Stars\nL153: \nL154: 538 stars\nL155: \nL156: ### Watchers\nL157: \nL158: 7 watching\nL159: \nL160: ### Forks\nL161: \nL162: cite75†44 forks L163: \nL164: cite76†Report repository L165: \nL166: main\nL167: \nL168: cite77†Branches cite78†Tags L169: \nL170: [Input: Go to file]\nL171: \nL172: Go to file\nL173: \nL174: Code\nL175: \nL176: Open more actions menu\nL177: \nL178: ## Latest commit\nL179: \nL180: \nL181: \nL182: ## History\nL183: \nL184: 1 Commit\nL185: ## Folders and files\nL186: Name  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\nL187: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL188: cite79†csrc | cite79†csrc |    |\nL189: cite80†lplb | cite80†lplb |    |\nL190: cite81†scripts | cite81†scripts |    |\nL191: cite82†tests | cite82†tests |    |\nL192: cite83†.clang-format | cite83†.clang-format |    |\nL193: cite84†.editorconfig | cite84†.editorconfig |    |\nL194: cite85†.gitignore | cite85†.gitignore |    |\nL195: cite86†.pre-commit-config.yaml | cite86†.pre-commit-config.yaml |    |\nL196: cite87†LICENSE | cite87†LICENSE |    |\nL197: cite88†README.md | cite88†README.md |    |\nL198: cite89†download-mathdx.sh | cite89†download-mathdx.sh |    |\nL199: cite90†pyproject.toml | cite90†pyproject.toml |    |\nL200: cite91†setup.py | cite91†setup.py |    |\nL201: [Button: View all files]\nL202: ## Repository files navigation\nL203: \nL204:   *   * cite92†README L205:   * cite92†MIT license L206: \nL207: More items\nL208: # Linear-Programming-Based Load Balancer (LPLB)\nL209: LPLB is a parallel load balancer that leverages linear programming to optimize expert parallel workload distribution for MoE (Mixture-of-Experts) models. It dynamically reorders experts based on workload statistics, constructs replicas considering static topology, and solves optimal token assignments for each batch to achieve dynamic load balancing.\nL210: The reordering process is facilitated by cite93†EPLB , and real-time workload statistics can be provided by the user, collected via `torch.distributed`, or obtained through the internal communicators of a cite94†Deep-EP buffer. Its embedded LP solver implements single-SM Interior Point Method (IPM) and leverages NVIDIA's cite95†cuSolverDx†developer.nvidia.com and cite96†cuBLASDx†developer.nvidia.com libraries for efficient linear algebra operations.\nL211: LPLB is currently in the early research stage, and performance improvements are still under evaluation.\nL212: ## Installation\nL213: \nL214: Prerequisites:\nL215: \nL216:   * CUDA Toolkit >= 12.6.3 (with cuSolverDx dependencies).\nL217:   * DeepEP is optional but strongly recommended for practical use.\nL218:   * EPLB is embedded.\nL219: \nL220:     ./download-mathdx.sh\nL221:     # export NVSHMEM_DIR=...  # Optional\nL222:     pip install --no-build-isolation .\nL223: \nL224: For testing, an editable installation is recommended:\nL225: \nL226:     pip install --no-build-isolation --editable .\nL227:     pytest tests\nL228: ## Interface and Example\nL229: \nL230:     # Global successes counter\nL231:     avail_counter = torch.zeros(1, dtype=torch.int64, device=\"cuda\")\nL232:     # Define topology of redundant experts\nL233:     r2o = torch.tensor(\nL234:         [\nL235:             [3, 0, 1, 2, 7, 4, 5, 6],\nL236:             [6, 7, 4, 5, 0, 1, 2, 3],\nL237:         ]\nL238:     ).T.int().cuda()\nL239: \nL240:     planner = Planner(\nL241:         r2o,\nL242:         n_logical_experts + n_redundants_per_rank * ep_size,\nL243:         n_logical_experts,\nL244:         group=ep_group,\nL245:     )\nL246:     # Initialize from a DeepEP `buffer` (optional)\nL247:     # planner.init_from_deep_ep(buffer)\nL248: \nL249:     N_SMS = 100\nL250:     # Logical expert indices selected by the model\nL251:     indices = ...\nL252:     # Planner returns physical expert indices\nL253:     redirected_indices = planner.run(indices, avail_counter, N_SMS)\nL254: ## How LPLB Works\nL255: \nL256: LPLB extends EPLB (Expert Parallelism Load Balancer) to address dynamic load imbalance in Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) training. While EPLB handles static imbalances (e.g., consistently overloaded experts due to data distribution), LPLB targets per-batch fluctuations caused by small-batch randomness during training.\nL257:   1. Redundant Experts: Each redundant expert is linked to an original expert, forming edges between GPUs.\nL258:   2. Edge Capacity: The capacity of an edge is the number of tokens assigned to its redundant expert in the current batch, defining the maximum token flow for balancing.\nL259:   3. LP Optimization: LPLB solves a linear programming (LP) problem to redistribute tokens along these edges, minimizing load imbalance within an expert-parallel (EP) group while respecting edge capacities.\nL260: Experts to be replicated are selected via EPLB (reordering only, no replication). The heaviest experts are then replicated based on the chosen LPLB topology. Real-time workload synchronization is optimized using NVLINK and NVSHMEM instead of `torch.distributed.allreduce`, reducing communication overhead. This requires DeepEP as a prerequisite.\nL261: ### Limitations\nL262:   * The current planner balances only total token count, not accounting for non-linearity in grouped matrix multiplication time costs, which may lead to suboptimal performance.\nL263:   * The solver takes ~100 µs for intra-node optimization (longer for inter-node), which may be non-negligible for small batches.\nL264:   * Under extreme global load imbalance, LPLB may perform worse than EPLB due to differences in assigning redundant experts (LPLB avoids assigning multiple replicas to the same original expert).\nL265: ### Typical Topologies\nL266:   * Cube: Replicates experts on a subset of GPUs, forming a cube graph with diagonal edges. Requires at least 2 experts per GPU. Ideal for balancing within an 8-GPU EP subgroup without sacrificing inter-node communication.\nL267:   * Hypercube: Similar to Cube but excludes diagonal edges and requires 16 GPUs. Suitable for expert parallelism across 16 GPUs.\nL268:   * Torus: Replicates one expert on a neighbor GPU in the same node and another on a neighbor node, forming a torus graph. Requires at least 2 experts per GPU. Effective for global balancing but less efficient due to intra-node communication than Cube.\nL269: Custom topologies can be explored by modifying the `r2o` matrix.\nL270: ## About\nL271: \nL272: An early research stage expert-parallel load balancer for MoE models based on linear programming.\nL273: \nL274: ### Resources\nL275: \nL276: cite71†Readme L277: \nL278: cite72†MIT license L279: \nL280: cite73†Activity L281: \nL282: cite74†Custom properties L283: \nL284: ### Stars\nL285: \nL286: 538 stars\nL287: \nL288: ### Watchers\nL289: \nL290: 7 watching\nL291: \nL292: ### Forks\nL293: \nL294: cite75†44 forks L295: \nL296: cite76†Report repository L297: \nL298: ## Releases\nL299: \nL300: ## Packages\nL301: \nL302: ## Contributors\nL303: \nL304: ## Languages\nL305: \nL306: You can’t perform that action at this time.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMiniMax 研究 - 技术分享与产品洞察 | MiniMax (https://www.minimaxi.com/blog)\nciteturn6578view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.minimaxi.com/blog\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://www.minimax.cn/blog; Total lines: 68\nL0: 模型\nL1: \nL2: cite0†Image: 语言模型 语言模型\nL3: \nL4: cite1†MiniMax M3 cite2†MiniMax M2.7 cite3†MiniMax M2.5 L5: \nL6: cite4†Image: 视频生成 视频生成\nL7: \nL8: cite5†MiniMax H3 L9: \nL10: cite6†Image: 语音&音乐 语音&音乐\nL11: \nL12: cite7†MiniMax Speech 2.8 cite8†MiniMax Music 3.0 L13: \nL14: 产品\nL15: \nL16: cite9†MiniMax Code†agent.minimax.cn cite10†MiniMax Design†design.minimax.cn cite11†语音 cite12†星野†www.xingyeai.com L17: \nL18: API\nL19: \nL20: 订阅\nL21: \nL22: 研究\nL23: \nL24: 关于我们\nL25: \nL26: cite13†Image: 与所有人共创智能 与所有人共创智能\nL27: \nL28: cite14†公司介绍 cite15†新闻 cite16†投资者关系†ir.minimax.cn cite17†加入我们 L29: \nL30: cite18†EN†www.minimax.io L31: \nL32: [Input]\nL33: \nL34: 模型\nL35: \nL36: cite0†Image: 语言模型 语言模型\nL37: \nL38: cite1†MiniMax M3NEW cite2†MiniMax M2.7 cite3†MiniMax M2.5 L39: cite4†Image: 视频生成 视频生成\nL40: \nL41: cite5†MiniMax H3NEW L42: \nL43: cite6†Image: 语音&音乐 语音&音乐\nL44: \nL45: cite7†MiniMax Speech 2.8NEW cite8†MiniMax Music 3.0NEW L46: \nL47: 产品\nL48: \nL49: cite9†MiniMax CodeNEW†agent.minimax.cn cite10†MiniMax Design†design.minimax.cn cite11†语音 cite12†星野†www.xingyeai.com L50: \nL51: API\nL52: \nL53: 订阅\nL54: \nL55: 研究\nL56: \nL57: 关于我们\nL58: \nL59: cite13†Image: 与所有人共创智能 与所有人共创智能\nL60: \nL61: cite14†公司介绍 cite15†新闻 cite16†投资者关系†ir.minimax.cn cite17†加入我们 L62: \nL63: cite18†English†www.minimax.io L64: # MiniMax 研究\nL65: \nL66: MiniMax 博客频道，分享人工智能技术文章、产品更新、最佳实践和行业洞察。\nL67: cite8†MiniMax Music 3.0Music Generation 2026-08-13 MiniMax Music 3.0：新一代开放权重、生产级全能音乐模型今天，我们正式推出新一代音乐生成模型 MiniMax Music 3.0。只需提供一个创意想法和可选的歌词，模型即可在一次生成中完成作曲、编曲、演唱与制作，创作出一首完整的歌曲。阅读更多 cite5†AIVideo Generation 2026-07-31 MiniMax H3：打破任务和模态的边界今天，我们正式发布 MiniMax H3。这是一款通用的全模态生成模型，支持对文本、图像、视频、声音组成的多模态上下文的统一理解能力、能够输出具备原生双声道的音视频，最高可支持 15s 2K 分辨率。阅读更多 cite19†AIM3 2026-06-09 MaxProof: 生成式验证强化学习驱动的数学证明进化系统在 M3 发布推文中，我们汇报了 M3 模型在 IMO 2025 与 USAMO 2026 两组国际数学竞赛真题上的表现：在搭配 MaxProof 框架后，M3 均超过了人类金牌线。本文将进一步展开我们在推进数学证明能力过程中的技术路径，包括基座模型能力增强、verifier 对齐、refinement 能力构建，以及测试时增强框架 MaxProof 的设计。阅读更多 cite20†AIM3 2026-06-01 MiniMax M3：前沿 Coding 能力，1M上下文，原生多模态，一个模型全给你编程和智能体任务上达到了前沿的能力。它是一个使用全新注意力架构 MSA （MiniMax Sparse Attention ），最高支持 1M 超长上下文。 同时，如外界所期待的那样，它也是一个原生多模态模型。阅读更多 cite21†AI大模型2026-05-25为什么MiniMax大模型不认识\"马嘉祺\"？Minimax内部排查稀疏 Token 遗忘问题的记录MiniMax M2 系列受到了开发者社区的广泛关注，不少用户在深度使用中发现了一些 corner case——其中\"模型无法说出马嘉祺\"这个问题引发了较多讨论。本文详细记录了Minimax团队的内部排查过程，从 tokenizer 对齐到 lm_head 退化分析，揭示了稀疏 Token 遗忘的机制及修复方案。阅读更多 cite22†AIAgent 2026-04-27 MiniMax Agent Team: 为长程任务，持续进化而生今天我们介绍 MiniMax Agent 的整体升级，我们将升级后的 Agent 起了个新名字：Mavis — MiniMax as a Jarvis，你的 AI 管家。阅读更多 cite23†AIM2.7 2026-03-18 MiniMax M2.7: 开启模型的自我进化在 M2 系列模型发布后的几个月，我们收到了大量热心用户的反馈和建议，这促使我们进一步加速模型的迭代效率。除了更加认真工作之外，我们能找到的唯一途径就是开启模型和组织的自我进化。MiniMax M2.7是我们第一个模型深度参与迭代自己的模型。阅读更多 cite24†AI强化学习2026-02-12 Forge: 可扩展的 Agent 强化学习框架与算法把强化学习推到真实世界的 Agent 上,会撞到一个三角约束:系统吞吐、训练稳定性、Agent 灵活性三者互相拉扯,长期阻碍着大规模 RL 在工业级系统中的落地。阅读更多 cite25†AILLM 2026-02-12 MiniMax M2.5: 更快更强更智能，为真实世界生产力而生今天我们来介绍 MiniMax-M2.5。经过数十万个真实复杂环境中的大规模强化学习训练，M2.5 在编程、工具调用和搜索、办公等生产力场景都达到或者刷新了行业的 SOTA，比如 SWE-Bench Verified（80.2%），Multi-SWE-Bench（51.3%），BrowseComp（76.3%）。M2.5 优化了模型对复杂任务的拆解能力和思考过程中 token 的消耗，使其能更快地完成复杂的 Agentic 任务，比如在 SWE-Bench Verified 的测试中，比上一个版本 M2.1 完成任务的速度快了 37%。阅读更多 cite26†AIRole-Play 2026-01-28 MiniMax-M2-her 技术深度解析Worlds to Dream, Stories to Live｜一份关于如何在生产环境中构建真正可用的 Role-Play Agent 的技术报告。今年是我们做 Talkie / Xingye 的第三年。三年时间，足以让一个产品在用户生命中留下痕迹，也足以让我们从长期的使用反馈中读出一些不那么显而易见的洞见。阅读更多 cite27†AICoding 2025-12-23 MiniMax M2.1: 大幅提升多语言编程，为真实世界复杂任务而生MiniMax M2.1 系统性提升了 Rust、Java、Golang、C++ 等多编程语言能力，强化 Web/App 开发美学与复合指令约束，在 SWE-bench、VIBE 等基准上接近 Claude Opus 4.5 水平，并在 Claude Code、Cline、Roo Code 等主流 Agent 框架中展现稳定泛化表现，为真实世界复杂办公与编程任务提供高效解决方案。阅读更多 cite28†AIAgent 2025-10-27 MiniMax M2 & Agent，大巧若拙MiniMax正式开源并上线MiniMax M2，专为Agent和代码而生，仅Claude Sonnet 8%价格，2倍速度。模型具备顶级代码能力、强大Agentic表现和极致性价比，在Artificial Analysis榜单排名全球前五。同时推出MiniMax Agent产品，提供Lightning高效模式和Pro专业模式，限时免费开放使用。阅读更多 cite29†MiniMax-01开源模型2025-01-15 MiniMax-01开源：新架构开启Agent时代MiniMax发布开源MiniMax-01系列模型，包含Text-01和VL-01。采用线性注意力架构，4560亿参数459亿激活，400万Token上下文是GPT-4o的32倍，极致性价比1元/百万Token起。MiniMax阅读更多 --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://agent.minimax.io/docs/llms.txt)\nciteturn6578view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/plain; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://agent.minimax.io/docs/llms.txt\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 54\nL0: # MiniMax Agent Docs\nL1: - [Welcome to MiniMax Code](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/welcome.md): MiniMax Code is a desktop AI Agent app for software development, everyday workflows, automation, and remote collaboration.\nL2: - [Download and Install](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/get-started/download.md): Download MiniMax Code and install it on macOS or Windows.\nL3: - [Create Your First Task](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/get-started/first-task.md): Start from the composer, attach context, and let MiniMax Code begin working.\nL4: - [Coding and Work Modes](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/workflows/modes.md): Understand when to use Coding mode or Work mode in MiniMax Code.\nL5: - [Workspace and Project Context](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/workflows/workspace.md): Attach a local project so the Agent can understand your files, scripts, and conventions.\nL6: - [Tasks and History](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/workflows/tasks.md): Manage tasks, history, pinned items, and archived conversations in MiniMax Code.\nL7: - [Conversation Collaboration](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/workflows/conversation-collaboration.md): Quote, comment on, and rate Agent replies directly in a conversation.\nL8: - [Code Review](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/workflows/code-review.md): Use the built-in Review command to inspect local code changes.\nL9: - [Permissions and Safety](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/workflows/permissions.md): Understand how MiniMax Code handles permission prompts for local files, commands, and external actions.\nL10: - [Infinite Canvas](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/desktop/infinite-canvas.md): Bring your assets, ideas, and generated results onto one canvas, and create alongside your Agent.\nL11: - [Goal](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/desktop/goal.md): Set a verifiable outcome for MiniMax Code and let it keep working until the goal is achieved or progress is blocked.\nL12: - [Built-in Browser](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/desktop/browser.md): Open pages, preview generated HTML, and debug web work inside the MiniMax Code task view.\nL13: - [Computer Use](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/desktop/computer-use.md): Let MiniMax Code operate apps on your computer directly for tasks that need a graphical interface.\nL14: - [Files / Changes / Terminal Panels](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/desktop/panels.md): Use MiniMax Code panels to inspect files, code changes, and command output.\nL15: - [Appearance](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/desktop/appearance.md): Adjust the display mode, theme, density, fonts, and colors of MiniMax Code.\nL16: - [Shortcuts and Mini Window](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/desktop/shortcuts.md): Use global shortcuts, command palette, and the mini window to work faster.\nL17: - [Updates and Feedback](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/desktop/updates-feedback.md): Check for MiniMax Code updates and submit feedback tickets from the desktop app.\nL18: - [Agent Team](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/agents/team.md): Use Agent Team for complex tasks that benefit from decomposition, collaboration, and verification.\nL19: - [Custom Agents](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/agents/custom-agents.md): Create dedicated Agents with their own role, instructions, skills, workspace, and channels.\nL20: - [Custom Mini Apps](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/agents/mini-apps.md): Bring information, tools, and everyday tasks together in one interface.\nL21: - [MiniApp Overview](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/agents/miniapp-overview.md): What MiniApp is, how it runs and is published, what is planned next, and two official examples: stocks and road trip planning.\nL22: - [H3 Max Video Generation](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/agents/h3-max.md): Create videos with sound from text prompts and reference media in MiniMax Code.\nL23: - [Plugin Marketplace](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/agents/plugins.md): Install and use domain plugins in MiniMax Code.\nL24: - [Plugin Development and Submission Guide](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/agents/plugin-submission.md): Capability choices, package layout, manifest fields, Skills and MCP servers, size limits, and the submission process for MiniMax Plugins.\nL25: - [MCP Servers](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/agents/mcp.md): Connect external tools and data sources to MiniMax Code.\nL26: - [Memory](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/agents/memory.md): Let MiniMax Code retain useful preferences, project conventions, and long-term working patterns.\nL27: - [Scheduled Tasks](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/automation/schedules.md): Let Agents run recurring work on a schedule.\nL28: - [Remote Control](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/automation/remote-control.md): Connect to your desktop Agent from your phone to check progress, add instructions, and approve permissions.\nL29: - [Messaging Integrations](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/automation/im.md): Collaborate with Agents through Telegram, WeChat, Lark, or Feishu depending on the locale.\nL30: - [M Plan and Credits](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/account/usage.md): View your MiniMax Code subscription plan, credit balance, and usage quotas.\nL31: - [MiniMax API Key](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/account/minimax-api.md): Configure your own MiniMax API key in MiniMax Code.\nL32: - [Custom Models / BYOK](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/account/byok.md): Add a custom model provider with base URL, API key, API format, and model names.\nL33: - [Configure BYOK Models with an Agent](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/account/byok-agent-setup.md): Configure custom models with an agent and verify real responses using curl on macOS and Windows.\nL34: - [FAQ](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/help/faq.md): Frequently asked questions about MiniMax Code desktop.\nL35: - [Feedback](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/help/feedback.md): Submit reproduction details, screenshots, and diagnostics when reporting issues.\nL36: - [Quick Start](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/cli/quick-start.md): Install MiniMax Code CLI, sign in, and complete your first terminal task.\nL37: - [Features and configuration](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/cli/features.md): Learn about MiniMax Code CLI entry points, interactive capabilities, configuration, and integrations.\nL38: - [Workflows](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/cli/workflows.md): Use MiniMax Code CLI for repository work, multi-turn tasks, plans, permissions, and session recovery.\nL39: - [Configuration and models](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/cli/configuration.md): Configure MiniMax Code CLI data directories, credentials, providers, models, proxies, skills, MCP, and plugins.\nL40: - [Headless and CI](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/cli/automation.md): Use mcode exec from Shell, CI, batch jobs, and evaluations with structured output and stable exit codes.\nL41: - [ACP integration](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/cli/integrations.md): Connect MiniMax Code CLI to editors and Agent hosts that support Agent Client Protocol.\nL42: - [Command reference](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/cli/reference.md): Review MiniMax Code CLI top-level commands, options, and machine-readable output entry points.\nL43: - [Security and permissions](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/cli/security.md): Understand MiniMax Code CLI permission modes, credential protection, and automation boundaries.\nL44: - [Troubleshooting](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/cli/faq.md): Troubleshoot MiniMax Code CLI installation, sign-in, configuration, automation, and terminal issues.\nL45: - [Changelog](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/changelog.md): Track every update to MiniMax Code.\nL46: - [Tech Blog](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/techblog.md): Technical articles from the MiniMax Agent engineering team.\nL47: - [Common Issues](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/question.md): Solutions for common MiniMax Code client startup, login, unresponsive app, and log upload issues.\nL48: - [Building a Testbed for Coding Agents](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/techblog/coding-agent-eval.md): Starting from MiniMax Code, building an evaluation system that can tell whether a Harness change actually helps.\nL49: - [I stopped babysitting my coding agents. Here's the workflow.](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/techblog/coding-agent-workflow-and-skills.md): What an audit of my multi-agent setup changed, and how I now define, verify and deliver work with core-grill, core-spec and deliver.\nL50: - [MiniMax Code Is Open Source: Inside the Harness Behind a Coding Agent](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/techblog/minimax-code-harness.md): Following one task through MiniMax Code: how each turn's capabilities are assembled, how context is managed, how tools are executed, how history is committed, and how sub-agent results are confirmed.\nL51: ## AsyncAPI Specs\nL52: \nL53: - [asyncapi](/docs/api-reference/speech/t2a/api/asyncapi.json)\n"
